\title{Using the \pkg{sts} Package (Version 1.7.1)}
\author{Shawn Mankad\\North Carolina State University \and Li Chen\\Cornell University}
\date{November 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

<<setup, echo=FALSE>>=
options(width = 80, digits = 4, continue = "  ", prompt = "R> ")
library(sts)
@

\begin{abstract}
The Structural Topic and Sentiment-Discourse (STS) model of
\citet{ChenMankad2025} is a topic model in which document-level covariates
determine both how much each topic is discussed (its prevalence) and how it is
discussed (a latent sentiment-discourse value for each topic in each
document). This vignette shows how to prepare text for the \pkg{sts} package,
fit the model, read the fitted object and its convergence record, estimate
the covariate effects with their uncertainty, and choose the estimation
settings. It describes version 1.7.1; the changes from earlier releases are
listed in the package's \code{NEWS} file and summarized in
Section~\ref{sec:versions}.
\end{abstract}

\section{The model}\label{sec:model}

A corpus has $D$ documents and a vocabulary of $V$ words. Document $d$ has
covariates $\bm x_d$ (with an intercept) and a vector of word counts. The STS
model has $K$ topics and, for each document, a latent vector
$\bm u_d=(\bm\eta_d,\bm\alpha_d)$ with
\begin{equation*}
\bm u_d \sim N(\bm\Gamma^\top\bm x_d,\ \bm\Sigma),\qquad
\bm\eta_d\in\mathbb R^{K-1},\quad \bm\alpha_d\in\mathbb R^{K}.
\end{equation*}
The topic proportions are $\bm\theta_d=\mathrm{softmax}(\bm\eta_d,0)$, so the
$K$-th topic is the reference. The entry $\alpha_{dk}$ is the
sentiment-discourse of document $d$ on topic $k$: it shifts topic $k$'s word
distribution,
\begin{equation*}
\beta_{kv}(\alpha_{dk}) \propto \exp\bigl(m_v + \kappa^{(t)}_{vk} + \kappa^{(s)}_{vk}\,\alpha_{dk}\bigr),
\end{equation*}
where $m_v$ is the log of word $v$'s overall frequency, $\kappa^{(t)}_{vk}$
is the topic's own deviation from it and $\kappa^{(s)}_{vk}$ says how word $v$
responds to sentiment-discourse on topic $k$. Each word of document $d$ is
drawn by choosing a topic from $\bm\theta_d$ and then a word from
$\bm\beta_k(\alpha_{dk})$. The regression coefficients $\bm\Gamma$ are the
quantities of interest in most applications: the first $K-1$ columns say how
the covariates move topic prevalence, the last $K$ how they move
sentiment-discourse.

\paragraph{Estimation.} \code{sts()} fits the model by the Laplace
variational EM algorithm of \citet{ChenMankad2025}:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{E-step.} For each document, $\hat{\bm u}_d$ maximizes the
log-posterior $f_d(\bm u_d)$ (by BFGS), and the variational covariance is
$(-\bm H_d)^{-1}$, the inverse of the negative Hessian at the mode.
\item \textbf{M-step for $\bm\Gamma$ and $\bm\Sigma$.} Each column of
$\bm\Gamma$ is estimated by the variational linear regression of \pkg{stm},
which shrinks the non-intercept coefficients toward zero, of the
$\hat{\bm u}_d$ on the covariates. $\bm\Sigma$ is then the average of the documents' covariances plus the spread
of the $\hat{\bm u}_d$ around their fitted means.
\item \textbf{M-step for $\bm\kappa$.} The expected word counts are
aggregated within the levels of the initialization variable, and each word's
coefficients are estimated by a Poisson regression with an $\ell_1$
(lasso) penalty, chosen by AIC along a regularization path. This is the
distributed multinomial regression approximation of \citet{Taddy2015}.
\end{itemize}
The fit is tracked through the approximate evidence lower bound of
\citet[Proposition~1]{ChenMankad2025},
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal B=\sum_d\Bigl[f_d(\hat{\bm u}_d)-\tfrac12\log\det(-\bm H_d)
-\tfrac12\log\det\bm\Sigma\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
reported without its additive constant. Section~\ref{sec:convergence}
explains why $\mathcal B$ does not rise at every iteration and how
\code{sts()} decides when to stop.

\section{Preparing the text}\label{sec:prep}

The examples use the \code{gadarian} data shipped with the package: 341
open-ended survey responses from an experiment on anxiety about immigration
\citep{Gadarian2014}, with a treatment indicator and the respondent's party
identification. \code{textProcessor()} lower-cases the text, removes
stop words, punctuation and numbers, and stems; \code{prepDocuments()}
builds the vocabulary and the sparse document representation, drops words
that appear in too few documents (\code{lower.thresh}) and drops documents
left empty. Both are ports of the functions of the same
names in \pkg{stm} \citep{Roberts2019}, so text prepared for \pkg{stm} can be
used directly.

<<prep>>=
temp <- textProcessor(documents = gadarian$open.ended.response,
                      metadata = gadarian, verbose = FALSE)
out <- prepDocuments(temp$documents, temp$vocab, temp$meta, verbose = FALSE)
length(out$documents); length(out$vocab)
@

Each element of \code{out\$documents} is a two-row integer matrix: vocabulary
indices in the first row and counts in the second. \code{out\$meta} holds
the covariates of the documents that were kept.

\paragraph{The initialization variable.} \code{sts()} needs one covariate,
\code{initializationVar}, whose levels are used to aggregate word counts in
the $\bm\kappa$ M-step and to initialize the sentiment-discourse values. It
is usually the main experimental or outcome variable: here the treatment,
coded $\pm1$; for online reviews, the star rating.

<<initvar>>=
out$meta$noTreatment <- ifelse(out$meta$treatment == 1, -1, 1)
@

\section{Fitting the model}\label{sec:fit}

The first argument gives the covariates of $\bm\Gamma$, the second the
initialization variable.

<<fit>>=
set.seed(2024)
fit <- sts(~ treatment * pid_rep, ~ noTreatment, out, K = 3, verbose = FALSE)
fit
@

<<fitinfo, echo=FALSE>>=
fitIters <- length(fit$elbo); fitBest <- fit$bestIter
fitTime <- round(fit$runtime, 1)
@

The fit ran \Sexpr{fitIters} iterations (\Sexpr{fitTime} seconds on one
thread of the machine that built this vignette) and returns iteration
\Sexpr{fitBest}, the one with the highest bound.
The main components of the fitted object are:
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{alpha}: the $D\times(2K-1)$ matrix of the $\hat{\bm u}_d$
(prevalence logits, then sentiment-discourse values);
\item \code{gamma}: $\hat{\bm\Gamma}$, labelled by covariate and topic, also
returned by \code{coef(fit)};
\item \code{kappa}: the list \code{kappa\_t}, \code{kappa\_s} of $V\times K$
matrices; \code{mv}: the $m_v$;
\item \code{sigma} and \code{sigma\_inv}: $\hat{\bm\Sigma}$ and its inverse;
\item \code{elbo}: $\mathcal B$ at every iteration; \code{bestIter}: the
iteration whose estimates are returned; \code{converged}: whether the
stopping test was met before \code{maxIter};
\item \code{diagnostics}: a data frame with, per iteration, $\mathcal B$, its
data-fit and Laplace terms, and the mean relative changes in $\bm\alpha$ and
$\bm\kappa$.
\end{itemize}

<<coef>>=
coef(fit, "sentiment")
@

\section{Convergence}\label{sec:convergence}

$\mathcal B$ is not monotone for this model, and \code{sts()} is designed for
that. The $\bm\kappa$ M-step maximizes a penalized Poisson approximation
rather than $\mathcal B$ itself, and the penalty is re-selected by AIC at
every iteration, so the bound can fall for one iteration or for many. (The
E-step maximizes $f_d$ rather than $f_d-\frac12\log\det(-\bm H_d)$, which can
lower it slightly too.) A test on the change over one iteration would stop
wherever one step happens to be small. \code{sts()} instead tracks the best
bound so far, $\mathcal B^*_t=\max_{s\le t}\mathcal B_s$, and stops at the
first iteration $t$ with
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mathcal B^*_t-\mathcal B^*_{t-W}}{0.1+|\mathcal B^*_{t-W}|}<\code{convTol},
\end{equation*}
where $W$ is \code{patience} (default 20) and \code{convTol} defaults to
$10^{-5}$. It returns the iterate with the highest bound. If the test is not
met within \code{maxIter} iterations (default 500), \code{sts()} warns and
sets \code{converged = FALSE}.

<<boundplot, fig=TRUE, width=6.5, height=3.2, include=FALSE>>=
d <- fit$diagnostics
par(mar = c(4, 4.5, 0.5, 0.5))
plot(d$iter, d$elbo, type = "l", xlab = "Iteration", ylab = "Bound")
abline(v = fit$bestIter, lty = 2)
@
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{usingSTS-boundplot}
\caption{The bound at each iteration of the Gadarian fit; the dashed line is
the returned iterate.}\label{fig:bound}
\end{figure}

<<nfalls>>=
sum(diff(fit$elbo) < 0)    # iterations in which the bound fell
@

Figure~\ref{fig:bound} shows the trajectory. The first iterations raise the
bound by far more than the later ones, and the later ones rise and fall by
small amounts. \code{allow.neg.change = TRUE} restores the rule of
\pkg{sts}~1.6 and earlier: stop at the first iteration whose relative change
is below \code{convTol}, including any decrease, and return the last iterate.

\section{Interpreting a fit}\label{sec:interpret}

\subsection{Topics and their words}

\code{printTopWords()} lists each topic's most likely words at low
sentiment-discourse (the 5th percentile of that topic's $\hat\alpha_{dk}$
over documents), at the average and at high sentiment-discourse (the 95th
percentile); \code{plot()} draws the same information with the word
probabilities (Figure~\ref{fig:words}).

<<topwords>>=
printTopWords(fit, n = 7)
@

<<wordplot, fig=TRUE, width=7, height=5, include=FALSE>>=
print(plot(fit, n = 7))
@
\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{usingSTS-wordplot}
\caption{\code{plot(fit)}: the most likely words of each topic at low, average
and high sentiment-discourse.}\label{fig:words}
\end{figure}

\code{topicSemanticCoherence()} and \code{topicExclusivity()} compute the two
topic-quality measures used with \pkg{stm} \citep{Mimno2011,Roberts2014}, on
the topic-word distributions at the average sentiment-discourse.
<<quality>>=
topicSemanticCoherence(fit, out)
topicExclusivity(fit)
@

\subsection{Representative documents}

\code{findRepresentativeDocs()} returns the \code{n} documents with the
highest proportion of a topic, with the quartile of each one's
sentiment-discourse on that topic, and \code{plotRepresentativeDocs()} draws
their text.
<<repdocs>>=
docs <- findRepresentativeDocs(fit, out$meta$open.ended.response, topic = 1, n = 4)
docs[, c("index", "sentiment_quartile")]
@

\subsection{Covariate effects}

The point estimates $\hat{\bm\Gamma}$ ignore the uncertainty in the
document-level variables. \code{estimateRegns()} accounts for it by the
method of composition \citep{Treier2008}, as \pkg{stm}'s
\code{estimateEffect()} does: it draws every document's $\bm u_d$ from a
normal distribution centred at $\hat{\bm u}_d$, regresses the topic
proportions and the sentiment-discourse values on the covariates, and pools
the draws over many such data sets. The draws share one covariance matrix,
the average of the documents' variational covariances, which is \pkg{stm}'s
``Global'' approximation.

<<regns>>=
set.seed(1)
regns <- estimateRegns(fit, ~ treatment * pid_rep, out)
printRegnTables(regns, topics = 1)
@

The result has \code{coef()}, \code{vcov()}, \code{confint()} and
\code{as.data.frame()} methods.
<<confint>>=
confint(regns, topic = 1, type = "sentiment")
@
The tables depend on the random number generator, so set the seed for
reproducible results; \code{nsims} (default 25) and \code{nsims\_se}
(default 250) set the number of draws.

\subsection{Held-out likelihood and the number of topics}

\code{make.heldout()} removes a share of the words of some documents, and
\code{heldoutLikelihood()} evaluates the fitted model on them: the result is
the average over documents of each document's mean held-out log-likelihood
per word. Comparing it across values of $K$ is one way to choose the number
of topics.

<<heldout>>=
ho <- make.heldout(out$documents, out$vocab, seed = 1)
ho$meta <- out$meta
sapply(c(3, 5), function(K) {
  set.seed(2024)
  f <- sts(~ treatment * pid_rep, ~ noTreatment, ho, K = K, verbose = FALSE)
  heldoutLikelihood(f, ho$missing)$expected.heldout
})
@

\section{Estimation settings}\label{sec:settings}

Table~\ref{tab:args} lists the arguments of \code{sts()} that control
estimation. The defaults suit most uses; the rest of this section explains
the ones users most often change.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering\small
\caption{Estimation arguments of \code{sts()}.}\label{tab:args}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Argument & Default & Role\\
\midrule
\code{maxIter} & 500 & iteration cap\\
\code{convTol} & $10^{-5}$ & relative tolerance of the stopping test\\
\code{patience} & 20 & window $W$ of the stopping test\\
\code{allow.neg.change} & \code{FALSE} & \code{TRUE}: the stopping rule of 1.6\\
\code{initialization} & \code{"anchor"} & spectral (anchor-word) or STM initialization\\
\code{cores} & \code{getOption("sts.cores", 1)} & threads; results do not depend on it\\
\code{logZ} & \code{"auto"} & log-partition: exact sums or interpolated tables\\
\code{logZSpacing} & 0.01 & largest table step\\
\code{logZMargin} & 2 & table range beyond the observed $\hat\alpha$\\
\code{kappaSolver} & \code{"cpp"} & $\bm\kappa$ M-step solver (\code{"glmnet"}: as in 1.6)\\
\code{estepOptimizer} & \code{"bfgs"} & E-step optimizer (\code{"newton"} also available)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Threads.} \code{cores} sets the number of threads for the E-step
(parallel over documents), the $\bm\kappa$ M-step (parallel over words) and
the spectral initialization. The work is divided into blocks that depend
only on the data, and partial results are combined in a fixed order, so a
fit is identical for any number of threads. \code{options(sts.cores = n)}
sets the default for a session.

\paragraph{The log-partition functions.} Each E-step needs, for every topic,
$\log Z_k(a)=\log\sum_v\exp(m_v+\kappa^{(t)}_{vk}+\kappa^{(s)}_{vk}a)$ and
its first two derivatives. Within an E-step these are fixed functions of the
scalar $a$, shared by all documents. \code{logZ = "exact"} sums over the
vocabulary at every evaluation; \code{"interpolated"} tabulates them once per
E-step and interpolates with quintic Hermite polynomials, so the cost per
document no longer depends on $V$. The table's step for topic $k$ is the
smaller of \code{logZSpacing} and $0.1/S_k$, where
$S_k=\max_v|\kappa^{(s)}_{vk}|$: the interpolation error depends on the step
only through $hS_k$, so this keeps it at the same small relative size when a
word's coefficient becomes large, as it can on a small corpus fitted for many
iterations. Values outside the table are computed exactly. The default
\code{"auto"} decides for each topic at every E-step: it tabulates a topic
when that is cheaper than summing over the vocabulary for every document
(about 20 evaluations each, with a factor of 4 to spare), and uses the exact
sums otherwise. The break-even point grows with $D$: with the 341 Gadarian
documents a topic is tabulated while its table needs fewer than about 1,700
nodes, which holds at the default step and fails once $S_k$ is large; on a
corpus of tens of thousands of documents the tables are used throughout, and
the saving grows with $D$.

\paragraph{Initialization.} \code{initialization = "anchor"} (the default)
initializes the topics by the spectral anchor-word method of
\citet{Arora2013}, as in \pkg{stm}, and the sentiment-discourse values from
the centred initialization variable. \code{initialization = "stm"} fits a
structural topic model with the initialization variable as its content
covariate and initializes $\bm\kappa$ from its word effects; a prefit
\pkg{stm} model can be passed as \code{stmSeed}.

\paragraph{Solvers.} The $\bm\kappa$ M-step uses a Poisson-lasso solver
written for the aggregated problem, which reproduces \pkg{glmnet}'s
regularization path and AIC selection \citep{Friedman2010} and solves each
problem to a tight tolerance; \code{kappaSolver = "glmnet"} uses
\pkg{glmnet} itself. \code{estepOptimizer = "newton"} replaces BFGS by
Newton's method with the exact Hessian, which needs fewer evaluations and is
faster when \code{logZ = "exact"}.

\section{Versions and reproducibility}\label{sec:versions}

Version 1.7 made estimation much faster and changed several defaults;
1.7.1 corrected the reported bound, the stopping rule, the accuracy of the
tables at large $S_k$ and the covariance used by \code{estimateRegns()}. The
\code{NEWS} file lists every change and its effect on results. In short:
\begin{itemize}
\item Up to 1.7.0, the reported bound used the term
$+\frac14\log\det(-\bm H_d)$ instead of Proposition~1's
$-\frac12\log\det(-\bm H_d)$. The estimates at a given iteration did not
depend on it, but the stopping point and the returned iterate did.
\code{options(sts.bound = "1.6")} restores the old term.
\item Up to 1.7.0, \code{estimateRegns()} drew with $\hat{\bm\Sigma}$, the
prior covariance, which overstates the uncertainty in the document-level
variables. \code{options(sts.regnsSigma = "prior")} restores it.
\item 1.7 removed a half-step damping of the $\bm\kappa$ update that 1.1--1.6
applied, and replaced a non-converging solver in the anchor-word
initialization; \code{options(sts.kappaDamping = 0.5)} and
\code{options(sts.recoverL2 = "expgrad")} restore them.
\end{itemize}
A fit of \pkg{sts}~1.6 is reproduced, up to floating-point reassociation, by
<<recipe16, eval=FALSE>>=
options(sts.kappaDamping = 0.5, sts.recoverL2 = "expgrad", sts.bound = "1.6")
fit16 <- sts(~ treatment * pid_rep, ~ noTreatment, out, K = 3,
             kappaSolver = "glmnet", logZ = "exact",
             allow.neg.change = TRUE, maxIter = 100)
@
\code{options(sts.regnsSigma = "prior")} gives \code{estimateRegns()} the
covariance of earlier releases, but their tables are not reproduced exactly:
1.7 fixed a bug in the prevalence tables of 1.6 and earlier, and the random
stream differs. Results agree across platforms up to rounding, except that
AIC selection is discontinuous at near-ties, so a rounding difference
occasionally changes the penalty chosen for a word and, through it, the
later iterates.

